  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B053",
    "B047"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B053 to B046. The round-1 exchange sharpens the decisive advantage of optical lattice clocks: the magic-wavelength method is a distinct physical invention, its performance has been independently reproduced, and it created a durable instrument for precision measurement and tests of fundamental physics. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's defining realization form the most coherent restricted attribution among the leading experimental cases. This is an experimentally verified door-opening method, not recognition for prospective standard-setting.",
    "case_against": "We must still verify that Katori and Ye alone appropriately delimit the original lattice-clock invention and realization, rather than the broader optical-clock lineage. My former preference, B053, remains a mature and important device invention, but its proposed credit spans enabling epitaxy, device concept, and first realization in a way that is presently less secure. B046 should prevail only with a citation kept strictly to optical lattice clocks.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength concept, defining high-performance realization, and independent reproducibility make optical lattice clocks an unusually clear experimental advance.",
        "response": "I agree, particularly with the distinction between the prize-worthy physical method and any future redefinition of the second. Independent performance across laboratories is the required confirmation that this is a mature instrument which genuinely permits new measurements; it also makes the Katori-Ye conceptual-and-experimental pairing more defensible than my B053 trio."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth stresses that lattice clocks unite quantum-control innovation with repeatedly tested performance and cautions against extending the citation to all optical-clock development.",
        "response": "I agree with both points. The achievement is sufficiently self-contained without claiming the entire optical-frequency-clock field, and that narrow framing answers my earlier concern that recognition might reward a broad later platform instead of the original experiment."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance but identifies priority and the division among early experiments, materials development, and device engineering as central vulnerabilities.",
        "response": "That is an important comparative lesson for B053 as well: long experimental maturity cannot substitute for a clean original-credit record. B046 has strong maturity while appearing to offer a more delimited discovery-to-realization chain; archival confirmation of that appearance remains essential before voting."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone appropriately represent the original lattice-clock invention and its defining realization.",
      "Whether the final citation can remain confined to optical lattice clocks without absorbing broader optical-clock development."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
